questions.q:
Given the following instance of a relation COURSE_ENROLLMENT (StudentID, CourseCode, Semester, Grade), identify which of the following attribute sets are candidate keys, and justify each:
(a) {StudentID, CourseCode}
(b) {StudentID, CourseCode, Semester}
(c) {StudentID}
(d) {CourseCode, Semester}

questions.a:
**Expected schema (one valid solution):**  
**BUS**(RegNo, Capacity, **DriverID** → DRIVER.EmpID)  
&nbsp;&nbsp;Candidate keys: {RegNo}  
**DRIVER**(EmpID, Name, LicenseNo)  
&nbsp;&nbsp;Candidate keys: {EmpID}, {LicenseNo} → Primary key: EmpID, Alternate key: LicenseNo  
**ROUTE**(RouteID, StartPoint, EndPoint, **BusRegNo** → BUS.RegNo)  
&nbsp;&nbsp;Candidate keys: {RouteID}  
**STUDENT_TRANSPORT**(AdmNo, **RouteID** → ROUTE.RouteID)  
&nbsp;&nbsp;Candidate keys: {AdmNo}  
A poor answer omits foreign keys, uses composite keys unnecessarily, or fails to list alternate keys (e.g., LicenseNo in DRIVER). This reveals the student cannot synthesise a schema from requirements — a core board exam skill.
